WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.220
A mouse cone is a light -detecting cell of the retina.

2
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:09.760
Its sensitivity resets as the background light brightens.

3
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.060
That reset is called adaptation.

4
00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:17.720
The textbook account rests on the best -known calcium sensors, the G -caps and

5
00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:21.620
recoverin. Delete them, and the cone still adapts.

6
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:23.220
So what is doing the work?

7
00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:28.010
Light closes channels in the outer segment, so calcium entry falls.

8
00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:31.830
A pump keeps removing calcium, so calcium falls.

9
00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:38.610
At low calcium, the G caps speed C -G -M -P synthesis, which reopens channels.

10
00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:44.430
Recoverin lets go of GRK1, the kinase that switches off activated pigment.

11
00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:48.390
Both push the cell back toward its dark state while the light stays on.

12
00:00:48.910 --> 00:00:50.930
That push is gain control.

13
00:00:51.783 --> 00:00:53.603
To test a loop, cut it.

14
00:00:53.923 --> 00:00:56.663
Vinberg and Kefalov recorded from isolated mouse retina.

15
00:00:57.243 --> 00:01:01.063
The mice also lacked rod transducin, so the signal came from cones.

16
00:01:01.743 --> 00:01:07.163
They compared control cones with cones lacking GCAP1, GCAP2 and recoverin.

17
00:01:07.603 --> 00:01:12.543
The knockout cones still adapted, even with bath calcium lowered to an estimated 30

18
00:01:12.543 --> 00:01:13.543
nanomolar.

19
00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:18.487
Sensitivity is response size divided by the strength of a dim flash.

20
00:01:19.007 --> 00:01:23.967
It falls as the background brightens, and a fit with two numbers describes the fall.

21
00:01:24.727 --> 00:01:29.327
I0 is the background that halves sensitivity, and n sets the slope.

22
00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:36.807
Control cones had I0 of 48 ,800 photons per square micrometre per second, and n of

23
00:01:36.807 --> 00:01:42.527
1. Cones lacking the sensors had 12 ,100 and n of 1 .5.

24
00:01:43.217 --> 00:01:48.677
At a background of 48 ,800, control cones keep half of their dark sensitivity.

25
00:01:49.657 --> 00:01:54.797
Cones lacking the sensors keep about 0 .11, each measured against its own dark

26
00:01:54.797 --> 00:02:00.757
sensitivity. To give a response of the same size, they need a flash 4 .55 times

27
00:02:00.757 --> 00:02:05.097
stronger. These are central fit values from mouse cones, xVivo.

28
00:02:05.537 --> 00:02:08.457
So the sensors matter, yet the cones still adapt.

29
00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:14.550
The authors read this as a calcium -independent component, and also reported a

30
00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:16.470
further calcium -dependent mechanism.

31
00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:20.650
But a low calcium bath changes the solution around the retina.

32
00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:27.310
It does not clamp calcium inside the outer segment, and faster C -G -M -P turnover

33
00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:29.930
falls short, with no molecule named.

34
00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:35.690
So keep this rule: G -caps and recover and contribute, and do not account for all of

35
00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:36.310
the adaptation.
